---------------------------------- Building advanced components -------------------------------------

- BUILD A REUSABLE ALERT COMPONENT WITH ng-content, INPUTS, OUTPUTS, AND DYNAMIC CLASSES.

- Instruction: Angular Prerequisite "Advanced components" chapter

    Our application has grown, and we start to feel the need to introduce a few UI components.
    For example, we display warnings and alerts in different parts of the application, but we never
    really tried to have a common component for them.

1️⃣ Let’s build an Alert!

        ng g c alert


2️⃣ The component’s template looks like the code we copy/pasted in several components:
    alert.html:

        <div role="alert">
            <button type="button" class="btn-close" aria-label="Close"></button>
        </div>

    🔥But this component will be slightly different from the other components we have built until
    now. This time we want to use our component to "wrap" the content that the alert should
    display, like this:

        <pr-alert>
            I'm an <strong>alert content</strong>
        </pr-alert>
    
    🔥The Alert should then use ng-content in its template, to display the wrapped content inside
    the div in its template…​

        <div class="alert" role="alert" >
            <button type="button" class="btn-close" aria-label="Close"></button>
            <ng-content />
        </div>



3️⃣ Now let’s add a few features. First we want to emit a closed event if our user clicks on the
    button. To do so, 🔥add a closeHandler method in your component, that emits a void event on an
    output named closed. 🔥This method should of course be called by a click on the button in our
    template.
    alert.ts:

        readonly closed = output<void>();
     👉protected closeHandler(): void {
            this.closed.emit();
        }
    
    alert.html:

        <div class="alert" role="alert">
            <button type="button" class="btn-close" aria-label="Close" 👉(click)="closeHandler()"></button>
            <ng-content />
        </div>



4️⃣ Then we need to style it a little. According to the official Bootstrap documentation, we need
    to add the CSS classes alert alert-danger for a "danger" alert, alert alert-success for a
    "success" alert, alert alert-warning for a "warning", etc. 🔥Let’s add an input to our component,
    called type with a default value 'warning': this will allow the other components to do
    something like:

        <pr-alert type="success">
            I'm a success alert content
        </pr-alert>

    You can type it as string, or to be more accurate, as a union type of the possible
    values: 'success' | 'danger' | 'warning'.
    alert.ts:

        readonly closed = output<void>();
        protected closeHandler(): void {
            this.closed.emit();
        }
     👉 readonly type = input<'success' | 'danger' | 'warning'>('warning');
    

    🔥Then add a computed field in the Alert class, called alertClasses, which returns
    'alert alert-danger' (as a dynamic string, or an array of strings) if the type is 'danger'
    for example. 🔥To finish, bind alertClasses to the class property of the root div in the template.
    alert.ts:

        readonly closed = output<void>();
        protected closeHandler(): void {
            this.closed.emit();
        }
        readonly type = input<'success' | 'danger' | 'warning'>('warning');
     👉 protected readonly alertClasses = computed(() => `alert-${this.type()}`)


    alert.html:

        <div class="alert" role="alert" 👉 [class]="alertClasses()">
            <button type="button" class="btn-close" aria-label="Close" (click)="closeHandler()"></button>
            <ng-content />
        </div>
 
 

 5️⃣ We’re almost feature complete! Some of the alerts we added in our app aren’t dismissible:
    we can’t close them. 🔥Let’s add another input called dismissible, with a default value true.
    🔥Use this signal in the template to display the closing button, only if the input has a true
    value. 🔥alertClasses must also include 'alert-dismissible' if the alert is dismissible.
    As a bonus, you can use the booleanAttribute transform for this input!
    alert.ts:

        readonly closed = output<void>();
        protected closeHandler(): void {
            this.closed.emit();
        }
        readonly type = input<'success' | 'danger' | 'warning'>('warning');
        protected readonly alertClasses = computed(() => 
         👉 [this.dismissible() ? 'alert-dismissible' : '', `alert-${this.type()}`] as const)
     👉 readonly dismissible = input(true, { transform: booleanAttribute });


    alert.html:

        <div class="alert" role="alert" [class]="alertClasses()">
         👉 @if (dismissible()) {
                <button type="button" class="btn-close" aria-label="Close" (click)="closeHandler()"></button>
            }
            <ng-content />
        </div>
    
    All right, our component is ready! Let’s now use it!



6️⃣ You can replace the alerts with our new component in Login, Register, Bet, Live, PendingRaces,
    and FinishedRaces. Remember to add to the imports it as well. 🔥Be careful to add the correct
    inputs (type of the alert, dismissible or not), and to listen to the closed event if necessary.
    bet.html:

        @if (betFailed()) {
            <!-- <div class="alert alert-danger alert-dismissible mb-3" role="alert">
            <button type="button" class="btn-close" aria-label="Close" (click)="betFailed.set(false)"></button>
            The race is already started or finished
            </div> -->
            <pr-alert class="mb-3" type="danger" ⚠️(closed)="betFailed.set(false)">
            The race is already started or finished
            </pr-alert>
        }
            // omitted
        @else if (raceModel.error()) {
            <!-- <div class="alert alert-danger" role="alert">An error occurred while loading the race.</div> -->
            <pr-alert type="danger" dismissible="false">An error occurred while loading the race.</pr-alert>
        }



7️⃣ Do not forget to copy all the test files and run ng test to be sure that everything is OK.